{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. It focuses on outcomes with probabilities below 20%, adjusting their weights upward by 10 percentage points (i.e., adding 0.10 to their objective probability) before computing a weighted sum of these low-probability outcomes. In Lottery A, the only outcome with a probability below 20% is none — both outcomes (6000 € at 25%, 0 € at 75%) are at or above 20%. In Lottery B, the 4000 € outcome has a 25% probability (above 20%), the 2000 € outcome has 25% (also above 20%), and the 0 € outcome has 50%. Therefore, no outcome in either lottery has a probability strictly below 20%. As a result, the adjustment rule (increasing weight by 10 percentage points for probabilities < 20%) does not apply to any outcome. The weighted sum of small-probability outcomes is thus zero for both lotteries under this rule. Hence, the decision reason would be indifferent between A and B, since no outcome qualifies for the adjustment and no differential preference can be formed based on this rule.",
